Hermann Zapf is probably the premier font designer in the world; he
did the font design for Euler (the font family commisioned by the AMS
for use with TeX).  Knuth added suggestions and did the technical
work, but the actual font and letterform design was all Zapf.

There's a rather interesting series of letters between Zapf and Knuth
that was published in Digital Typography (Knuth's book on the art of
computer typesetting); it's a reprint of an older article.  Google
says the original is "AMS Euler - A New Typeface for Mathematics.
Scholarly Publishing 20 (1989), 131-157".  It's probably easier to
find the book, and if you're interested in this kind of thing you'll
want the book anyway.

Knuth did the design for Computer Modern (the other main TeX font)
himself, and stayed away from anything really inventive as a
consequence.  It's a fairly generic modern serif font (sort of a
vanilla version Century Schoolbook).  I find it inoffensive and
readable but not particularly interesting.



